// a third-party subprocess changes the trust boundary rather than the wiring.
//
// The SINGLE credential-shaped value crossing host to provider is the
// authorization code, and that is unavoidable — the host receives it and the
// provider must exchange it. It is acceptable because the code is single-use,
// short-lived, PKCE-bound, and useless without the verifier the provider holds.
//
// # Nothing obtained from the exchange crosses back
//
